GitHub Actions and AWS CI/CD integration

Production incident scenarios

Real-time project-based interview questions

Hands-on Git commands

1. Git and GitHub fundamentals – interview questions
Q1. What is Git? Why do we use it in DevOps?
Answer:

Git is a distributed version control system used to manage source code changes.

In DevOps, we use Git to:

Maintain application and infrastructure code.

Track code changes and history.

Collaborate with multiple developers.

Create feature branches.

Review code using Pull Requests.

Integrate with CI/CD tools such as Jenkins and GitHub Actions.

Roll back code when deployment issues occur.

Real-time example:

In my project, developers push application code into GitHub feature branches. Once development is completed, they create Pull Requests against the development branch. After code review and approval, the code is merged.

Jenkins or GitHub Actions detects the merge and triggers the CI/CD pipeline to deploy the application to AWS environments.

Q2. What is the difference between Git and GitHub?
Git

GitHub

Version control system

Cloud-based Git hosting platform

Installed locally

Web-based platform

Tracks code changes

Hosts repositories

Supports branching and merging

Supports PRs and code reviews

Works offline

Requires connectivity for remote operations

Other Git hosting platforms include GitLab, Bitbucket and AWS CodeCommit.

Q3. What is the difference between Git pull and Git fetch?
Git fetch:

git fetch origin

Downloads changes from the remote repository but does not merge them into your current branch.

Git pull:

git pull origin main

Downloads changes and integrates them into your current branch. By default, it performs fetch followed by merge.

You can also use:

git pull --rebase origin main

This fetches and rebases your local commits on top of the remote branch.

Interview tip: Fetch is safer when you want to inspect remote changes before integrating them.

Q4. What is the difference between git clone and git fork?
git clone: Creates a local copy of an existing repository.

git fork: Creates a separate copy of a repository under your GitHub account or organization.

Forking is commonly used in open-source contribution workflows.

Q5. What is the difference between git add, git commit and git push?
git add .
git commit -m "Updated application configuration"
git push origin feature/config-update

git add: Moves changes into the staging area.

git commit: Saves staged changes in local Git history.

git push: Uploads local commits to a remote repository.

2. Git branching strategy – real-time interview questions
Q6. Which branching strategy have you used in your project?
Interview-ready answer:

In my project, we follow a GitFlow-style branching strategy with some adjustments based on our release process.

We maintain the following branches:

main / master

Production-ready code
develop

Integration branch for upcoming releases
feature/

New functionality
bugfix/

Bug corrections
release/

Release preparation
hotfix/

Urgent production fixes
Example workflow:

Developer creates a feature branch from develop.

Developer completes code changes.

Developer pushes the feature branch to GitHub.

Developer creates a PR against develop.

Team reviews and approves the PR.

Changes are merged into develop.

Release branch is created for testing.

After validation, release changes are merged into main.

Production deployment pipeline is triggered.

Q7. What is a feature branch?
A feature branch is a separate branch created to develop a specific functionality without modifying the main development branch.

git checkout develop
git pull origin develop
git checkout -b feature/payment-service

After development:

git add .
git commit -m "Implemented payment service"
git push origin feature/payment-service

Then create a PR.

Q8. What is a hotfix branch? When do you use it?
Scenario: An application is running in production, but a critical issue is identified.

We create a hotfix branch from the production branch:

git checkout main
git pull origin main

git checkout -b hotfix/payment-failure

Fix the issue, test it, commit and push:

git add .
git commit -m "Fixed payment failure"
git push origin hotfix/payment-failure

Create a PR against main, get approval, merge and deploy.

We also make sure the fix is integrated into develop so that the issue does not reappear in future releases.

3. Pull Request (PR) and GitHub – important interview questions
Q9. What is a Pull Request? Explain the complete PR process.
Answer:

A Pull Request is a request to merge code changes from one branch into another branch.

In my project, developers create PRs after completing their development work. We use PRs to ensure code quality, peer review, and approval before merging code into the integration or production branch.

Practical commands:

git checkout -b feature/user-service

# Make code changes

git add .
git commit -m "Added user service"
git push origin feature/user-service

Then in GitHub:

Open the repository.

Click Pull Requests.

Click New Pull Request.

Select base branch: develop.

Select compare branch: feature/user-service.

Add title and description.

Assign reviewers.

Create PR.

Resolve review comments.

Get approval and merge.

Q10. What happens when you create a PR?
When we create a PR, GitHub:

Compares source and target branches.

Displays file-level changes.

Shows commit history.

Runs configured CI checks.

Allows reviewers to comment.

Checks branch protection requirements.

Allows merging when the required conditions are satisfied.

Q11. What are the different merge strategies in GitHub?
Strategy

Explanation

When to use

Merge commit

Preserves branch history

When complete history is required

Squash merge

Combines commits into one

Clean and simple history

Rebase merge

Replays commits on target branch

Linear history

My practical approach: If our team wants a clean commit history for feature development, we commonly use squash and merge. However, the final choice depends on repository policy.

Q12. What is branch protection in GitHub?
Branch protection prevents unauthorized or unreviewed changes from entering important branches.

For production branches, we configure:

Require Pull Request before merging.

Require one or more approvals.

Require status checks to pass.

Prevent force pushes.

Prevent branch deletion.

Require conversation resolution.

Example: Developers cannot directly push code into main. They must create a PR and receive approval.

Q13. What is the difference between PR approval and PR merging?
Approval means the reviewer has accepted the proposed code changes.

Merge means the changes are integrated into the target branch.

Approval does not automatically mean the PR is merged.

Q14. Can you create a PR without committing?
No. GitHub PRs are based on commits pushed to a remote branch. We need to commit and push the changes before creating a PR.

Q15. What happens if two developers modify the same file?
A merge conflict may occur if Git cannot automatically combine the changes.

We need to manually resolve the conflicting sections, test the code, and complete the merge.

4. Git merge conflicts – scenario-based questions
Very important for 6-year experience
Q16. You are working on a feature branch. Another developer has merged changes into develop. How will you update your feature branch?
Scenario:

You are working on:

feature/payment-service

Meanwhile, another developer merged changes into:

develop.

You need to bring the latest develop changes into your feature branch.

Solution:

git checkout develop
git pull origin develop

git checkout feature/payment-service

git merge develop

If conflicts occur, check:

git status

Open the conflicting files and resolve the conflict markers.

Example:

<<<<<<< HEAD
Old payment implementation
=======
New payment implementation
>>>>>>> develop
After resolving:

git add .
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflicts with develop"
git push origin feature/payment-service

The existing PR will be updated.

Interview answer: I first fetch the latest changes from the remote branch, integrate them into my feature branch, resolve conflicts, run tests, and push the updated branch.

Q17. What will you do if a merge conflict occurs during a production release?
Answer:

First, I will not force the merge.

I will:

Identify the conflicting files.

Understand the changes from both branches.

Discuss with the respective developers if business logic is affected.

Resolve conflicts carefully.

Run unit and integration tests.

Validate the build.

Get the required approval.

Proceed with the merge and deployment.

For production releases, I will ensure the conflict resolution is tested in a non-production environment first.

Q18. How do you abort a merge if something goes wrong?
git merge --abort

This attempts to return the working tree to its state before the merge started.

For an in-progress rebase:

git rebase --abort

Q19. How do you resolve a conflict during a rebase?
git status

Edit the conflicting files and remove conflict markers.

Then:

git add <filename>
git rebase --continue

To cancel:

git rebase --abort

5. Important advanced Git commands
These are particularly important for experienced DevOps interviews.

Q20. What is the difference between git reset, git revert and git restore?
Command

Purpose

git reset

Moves HEAD and can modify staging/working tree

git revert

Creates a new commit that reverses an earlier commit

git restore

Restores file contents

Example 1: Undo the last commit but keep changes staged

git reset --soft HEAD~1

Example 2: Undo the last commit and unstage changes

git reset --mixed HEAD~1

Example 3: Remove the last commit and discard its tracked changes

git reset --hard HEAD~1

Be careful: --hard can permanently discard uncommitted changes.

Example 4: Revert a commit safely

git revert <commit-id>

This creates a new commit that reverses the specified commit.

Interview tip: In a shared production branch, I generally prefer git revert instead of git reset, because revert preserves the existing commit history.

Q21. What is cherry-pick?
Cherry-pick allows us to apply a specific commit from one branch to another branch.

Real-time scenario:

A developer fixed a critical bug in the develop branch, but we need only that particular fix in production.

First, identify the commit:

git log --oneline

Switch to the target branch:

git checkout main
git pull origin main

Apply the specific commit:

git cherry-pick <commit-id>

Push the change:

git push origin main

In a protected production branch, this should normally be done through a PR rather than pushing directly.

Q22. What is git rebase?
Rebase moves or replays commits from one branch onto another base commit.

Example:

git checkout feature/login
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/develop

This places your feature commits on top of the latest develop history.

Merge vs rebase:

Merge preserves branch history and creates a merge commit when needed.

Rebase creates a linear history by replaying commits.

Avoid rebasing shared branches because it rewrites commit history.

Q23. How do you identify who modified a particular line of code?
git blame filename

For example:

git blame application.yml

This shows the commit, author and date associated with each line.

Q24. How do you check commit history?
git log
git log --oneline
git log --graph --oneline --all

Q25. How do you find a particular commit?
git log --oneline
git show <commit-id>

You can also search commit messages:

git log --grep="payment"

6. Real-time troubleshooting scenarios
This is one of the most important sections for your experience level. Interviewers often give a problem and expect you to explain how you would investigate it.

Scenario 1: You pushed code to the wrong branch. What will you do?
Situation: You accidentally pushed feature code into the develop branch.

Answer:

First, I identify whether other developers have already pulled or built on those changes.

If the incorrect changes are already shared, I prefer reverting them.

git checkout develop
git pull origin develop

git revert <commit-id>
git push origin develop

Then I create the correct feature branch and apply the changes there if needed.

If the commit has not been shared, I may consider resetting the branch, subject to team approval.

Scenario 2: You committed a password or AWS access key and pushed it to GitHub. What will you do?
Production security incident
Answer:

This is a security incident.

My first priority is to invalidate the exposed credential.

Steps:

Immediately deactivate or rotate the exposed AWS access key in IAM.

Check CloudTrail for suspicious API activity.

Notify the security team.

Remove the secret from the current code.

Use AWS Secrets Manager or Systems Manager Parameter Store to manage secrets.

Review GitHub repository history and remove the secret from history if necessary.

Coordinate a force push if history rewriting is required.

Inform all developers to synchronize their local repositories.

Important: Deleting a secret from the latest commit does not remove it from Git history. Even after cleaning history, treat the credential as compromised.

Scenario 3: Your GitHub PR is showing merge conflicts. How will you troubleshoot?
Answer:

First, I check the target branch and source branch.

git fetch origin
git checkout feature/login
git merge origin/develop

Then:

git status

Resolve conflicting files, test the application and push the changes.

git add .
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflicts"
git push origin feature/login

The PR should now reflect the updated branch.

Scenario 4: Your Git push is rejected with a non-fast-forward error. How will you resolve it?
Error:

! [rejected] feature/login -> feature/login
(non-fast-forward)
Reason: Someone has pushed new commits to the remote branch that are missing from your local branch.

Solution:

git fetch origin
git pull --rebase origin feature/login

Resolve any conflicts, then:

git push origin feature/login

Avoid using git push --force as the first solution.

Scenario 5: You accidentally deleted a branch. Can you recover it?
Yes, if the relevant commit is still reachable through the reflog or another reference.

git reflog

Find the last commit associated with that branch.

Recover it:

git checkout -b feature/recovered <commit-id>

Then push it:

git push origin feature/recovered

Scenario 6: Your GitHub PR checks are failing. What will you check?
Answer:

I will check:

GitHub Actions workflow logs.

Build failures.

Unit test failures.

Code quality and security scan failures.

Dependency installation issues.

Branch protection rules.

Required status checks.

Environment and permission issues.

For example, if a Jenkins build is integrated with GitHub, I will inspect Jenkins console output and determine whether the failure is related to Git checkout, build dependencies, tests or deployment.

Scenario 7: A developer force-pushed to a shared branch and your commits disappeared. What will you do?
Answer:

I will not immediately push more changes.

First:

git reflog
git log --all --oneline

I will identify the missing commits and recover them into a separate branch.

git checkout -b recovery-branch <commit-id>

Then I will coordinate with the team to restore the required changes safely through a PR.

Scenario 8: Your GitHub Actions pipeline is not triggering after a code push. What will you check?
Answer:

I will check:

Workflow file exists under .github/workflows/.

YAML syntax is valid.

The on: trigger matches the branch being pushed.

Workflow is not disabled.

Repository Actions permissions.

Path filters and branch filters.

Whether the workflow is skipped because of commit-message conditions.

Organization-level Actions restrictions.

Example:

name: CI Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
      - develop
  pull_request:
    branches:
      - main
      - develop

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Build
        run: echo "Building application"

Scenario 9: Your PR was merged, but the application deployment failed. What will you do?
Answer:

I will separate the Git merge issue from the deployment issue.

My troubleshooting steps:

Confirm whether the merge completed successfully.

Identify the exact commit deployed.

Check GitHub Actions or Jenkins pipeline logs.

Identify whether the failure is in build, test, artifact publishing or deployment.

Check AWS deployment logs and application health.

If the release caused a production issue, execute the approved rollback procedure.

Fix the issue and create a new PR.

Important: Reverting a Git commit alone does not automatically roll back a deployed AWS application. The deployment pipeline must deploy the reverted version or a known-good artifact.

Scenario 10: A developer says their local code is different from GitHub. How will you troubleshoot?
git status
git branch -vv
git remote -v
git fetch origin
git log --oneline --graph --all

I will check:

Whether they are on the correct branch.

Whether local changes are uncommitted.

Whether they have pulled the latest remote changes.

Whether they are tracking the correct remote branch.

Whether their local branch has diverged.

Then I will synchronize the branch safely.

7. Git advanced concepts – 6 years experience
Q26. What is HEAD in Git?
HEAD is a reference to the currently checked-out commit, usually through the current branch.

git log --oneline -1
git rev-parse HEAD

Q27. What are the three states of Git?
Git has three important working states:

State

Meaning

Modified

File has changed but is not staged

Staged

File is ready for commit

Committed

Changes are saved in local repository history

Q28. What is the difference between local and remote branches?
Local branch: Exists in your local repository.

Remote-tracking branch: Represents the last known state of a remote branch.

Remote branch: Exists in the remote repository, such as GitHub.

Commands:

git branch
git branch -r
git branch -a

Q29. What is a Git tag?
A tag identifies a specific commit, commonly used for application releases.

Example:

git tag v1.0.0
git push origin v1.0.0

To create an annotated tag:

git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Production release 1.0"
git push origin v1.0.0

In DevOps, tags are useful for identifying which version of application code was deployed to production.

Q30. What is .gitignore?
.gitignore specifies files and directories that Git should ignore when detecting untracked files.

Example:

.env
*.log
node_modules/
target/
.terraform/
*.tfstate
Important: .gitignore does not automatically remove files already tracked by Git.

To stop tracking an existing file:

git rm --cached .env
git commit -m "Stop tracking environment file"

Q31. What is git stash?
Git stash temporarily saves uncommitted changes so that you can switch branches without committing incomplete work.

git stash
git checkout develop

Restore changes:

git stash list
git stash pop

Difference:

git stash pop: Applies changes and removes the stash entry if successful.

git stash apply: Applies changes but keeps the stash entry.

Q32. What is git bisect?
Git bisect helps identify which commit introduced a bug using binary search.

Example:

git bisect start
git bisect bad
git bisect good <known-good-commit>

Git checks out commits for testing.

Mark each tested commit:

git bisect good

or

git bisect bad

When finished:

git bisect reset

Real-time use: A production application started failing after a recent deployment, and we don't know which commit introduced the issue.

Q33. What is git reflog?
Git reflog records updates to references such as HEAD and branch movements in your local repository.

git reflog

It is useful when:

A branch was accidentally deleted.

A reset removed a commit from the branch.

You need to recover a previous HEAD position.

Q34. What is a bare repository?
A bare repository contains Git version-control data without a working directory.

It is commonly used as a central remote repository.

git init --bare project.git

Q35. What are Git hooks?
Git hooks are scripts executed at specific Git lifecycle events.

Examples:

Hook

Purpose

pre-commit

Validate code before commit

commit-msg

Validate commit message

pre-push

Run checks before pushing

post-merge

Execute tasks after merging

Example location:

.git/hooks/

In a DevOps project, we can use hooks to enforce commit-message standards, formatting and basic validation.

8. GitHub administration and security – interview questions
Q36. How do you manage GitHub repository access?
I follow the principle of least privilege.

Typical access levels:

Read: Can view and clone.

Triage: Can manage issues and PRs.

Write: Can push code and manage PRs.

Maintain: Can manage repository without sensitive administrative access.

Admin: Full repository administration.

In an organization, I prefer managing access through teams instead of assigning permissions individually.

Q37. What is the difference between GitHub authentication using HTTPS and SSH?
HTTPS:

git clone https://github.com/company/project.git

Authentication is generally done through a Personal Access Token or supported credential mechanism, not an account password.

SSH:

git clone git@github.com:company/project.git

Authentication uses an SSH key pair.

For automated CI/CD workflows, I prefer using short-lived credentials or appropriately scoped tokens wherever supported.

Q38. What is a GitHub Personal Access Token?
A PAT is a token used to authenticate GitHub operations.

It can be used for:

Repository access.

Git operations over HTTPS.

API operations.

Automation.

I prefer fine-grained tokens with minimum required repository permissions and an expiration date.

Q39. How do you protect sensitive information in GitHub?
My approach:

Never commit passwords or AWS access keys.

Use AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store.

Configure GitHub secret scanning.

Enable push protection where available.

Use IAM roles and OIDC for GitHub Actions to access AWS.

Apply least-privilege repository permissions.

Rotate exposed credentials immediately.

Q40. What is GitHub Actions?
GitHub Actions is a CI/CD automation platform integrated with GitHub.

It can automate:

Build.

Testing.

Code quality checks.

Docker image creation.

Image publishing to Amazon ECR.

Deployment to AWS.

Example workflow:

name: DevOps-CI

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - develop

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Build application
        run: |
          echo "Building application"

      - name: Run tests
        run: |
          echo "Running unit tests"

Q41. How do you integrate GitHub with AWS?
Interview-ready answer:

In our DevOps environment, we integrate GitHub with AWS to automate application deployments.

Typical architecture:

GitHub Repository

Developer pushes code / PR merged
GitHub Actions

Build, test and scan
AWS IAM + OIDC

Secure AWS authentication without long-lived AWS access keys
Amazon ECR

Store Docker images
Amazon ECS / EKS

Deploy application
We configure an IAM OIDC identity provider and a role with restricted trust conditions. GitHub Actions requests temporary AWS credentials using AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.

This avoids storing long-lived AWS access keys in GitHub secrets.

Q42. How do you trigger a deployment only after PR merge?
We can use a push trigger on the deployment branch.

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

This workflow runs when a push event updates main, including a PR merge that updates main.

For deployment workflows, we also use environment protection rules and approval requirements where appropriate.

Q43. What is CODEOWNERS in GitHub?
CODEOWNERS defines which users or teams should review changes to specific files or directories.

Example:

# Infrastructure code
/terraform/ @devops-team

# Application code
/src/ @backend-team

# Deployment configuration
/.github/workflows/ @devops-team
This helps ensure the appropriate team reviews sensitive infrastructure and pipeline changes.

9. AWS DevOps real-time project scenarios
These questions are especially useful because interviewers with 6 years of experience often want to understand how you handled actual project situations.

Q44. A developer merged code into main accidentally. How will you handle it?
Answer:

First, I will identify the merged commit and check whether deployment has started.

If the code is not suitable for production, I will:

Stop or pause the deployment pipeline if possible.

Assess the impact.

Create a revert PR.

Get the required approvals.

Merge the revert.

Deploy the previous stable version if necessary.

Inform the team and document the incident.

Command:

git revert -m 1 <merge-commit-id>

Here -m 1 specifies the mainline parent of the merge commit. I would verify the correct parent before executing it.

Q45. Your application deployment is successful, but the latest code is not visible. What will you check?
Answer:

I will investigate the entire deployment chain.

Was the correct branch checked out?

Was the correct commit built?

Was the new artifact published?

Was the correct Docker image tag used?

Was ECS/EKS updated with the new image?

Is the old image cached or still running?

Is traffic routed to the correct environment?

I will compare the Git commit SHA, Docker image digest and deployed application version to identify where the mismatch happened.

Q46. How do you maintain code quality before merging into production?
Answer:

We enforce PR checks such as:

Code review.

Unit tests.

Integration tests.

SonarQube quality gate.

Dependency vulnerability scans.

Container image scanning.

Terraform validation and security scanning.

Successful CI build.

We configure branch protection so required checks must pass before merging.

Q47. How do you handle multiple developers working on the same repository?
Answer:

We follow a defined branching strategy and code review process.

Each developer works on an independent feature branch.

Developers regularly synchronize with the target branch.

PRs require peer review.

CI checks run before merging.

Conflicts are resolved by the developers responsible for the affected code.

Only authorized users can merge into protected branches.

Q48. How do you rollback a production release using Git?
Answer:

If a release introduces a production issue, I first identify the exact problematic commit.

For a normal commit:

git revert <bad-commit-id>

For a merge commit:

git revert -m 1 <merge-commit-id>

Then I create a PR, get approval and merge it.

The deployment pipeline deploys the reverted code or a known-good artifact.

For urgent production recovery, I follow the organization's approved rollback process rather than bypassing branch protections.

Q49. What is the difference between GitHub Actions and Jenkins?
GitHub Actions

Jenkins

Integrated with GitHub

Separate automation server

GitHub-hosted or self-hosted runners

Agents and controllers

YAML-based workflows

Jenkinsfile / Groovy pipelines

Built-in GitHub event triggers

Webhooks and SCM polling

Managed service option

Self-managed infrastructure is common

In projects, both can integrate with AWS for CI/CD deployments.

Q50. How do you manage Terraform code using GitHub?
Answer:

We maintain Terraform code in GitHub and use feature branches and PR reviews.

Typical process:

Developer creates a Terraform feature branch.

Updates infrastructure code.

Creates a PR.

CI runs terraform fmt, terraform validate and security checks.

CI generates a Terraform plan.

Reviewers validate the proposed infrastructure changes.

After approval, the deployment workflow runs terraform apply with the appropriate permissions and state locking.

This ensures infrastructure changes are version-controlled and reviewed.

